<script>
	import Statement from '$lib/components/Statement.svelte';
	import Proof from '$lib/components/Proof.svelte';
	import * as factor from './statements/factor.md';
	import * as fullRank from './statements/full-rank.md';
	import * as forms from './statements/forms.md';
</script>

Integrating a quantity out of a model leaves a function of the quantities it was tied to, its
[Markov blanket](../generative-models/#the-dependence-graph). For a model of
[normals](../normal/) that function is Gaussian in shape, and it is tempting to write it as a
normal density of the blanket. A normal density has a covariance, though, and so can only describe
a function that pins down every direction of the blanket. Many do not. A landmark seen by bearing
from a single pose is the standard case: once the pose is integrated out, the bearing has said in
which direction the landmark lies, and nothing about how far. What is left is Gaussian across the
bearing and constant along it, and no covariance describes it. Forced into a normal, it must either
be refused or be given a depth that the data never supplied.

This note defines the function that needs neither: the *improper Gaussian factor*. It keeps exactly
what was said and nothing more, such factors add, and when one does determine its blanket it is a
normal.

## The factor

Let $\point_1, \dots, \point_k$ be points of manifolds of dimensions $\dimension_1, \dots,
\dimension_k$, each with a retraction, and fix for each an *anchor* $p_i$ in its manifold. The
*stacked tangent* is the vector of their coordinates in the charts at the anchors,

$$
	\delta(\point_B) = (\point_1 \ominus p_1,\ \dots,\ \point_k \ominus p_k) \in \R^D,
	\qquad D = \sum_i \dimension_i , @tag(stacked)
$$

the tuple standing, here and below, for the column vector with its entries stacked. The anchors
are fixed points, and do not move with the points the factor is a function of.

<Statement {...factor} />

The rows say what the factor determines. Write $\delta = \delta_\parallel + \delta_\perp$, with
$\delta_\parallel$ in the row space of $A$ and $\delta_\perp$ in its null space. Along the row
space the squares are a Gaussian. Along the null space the squares do not change, and the factor
is linear, with slope $-\eta\transpose \delta_\perp$. A factor with $r = 0$ rows is linear and a
constant alone.

The factor is a [likelihood factor](../generative-models/#conditioning-and-likelihoods): a
nonnegative function $e^\logterm$ of quantities of a model, which reweights the model's law and is
not the density of anything. It therefore needs no reference measure and no log-volume, even over
a curved or a positive quantity. Whatever measure an integral was taken in before the factor was
written is already in its values.

## When it is a normal

<Statement {...fullRank} />

<Proof>

If $A$ has rank less than $D$, take $n \ne 0$ with $A n = 0$. Along the line $\delta + t n$ the
factor is $\logterm(\delta) - t\, \eta\transpose n$, so $e^{\logterm}$ is constant or grows
exponentially along every such line, and the integral over $\R^D$ diverges. If $A$ has rank $D$,
$\Lambda = A\transpose A = W\transpose W$ is positive definite, and

$$
	\begin{aligned}
		-\tfrac12 \lvert A \delta - b \rvert^2 - \eta\transpose \delta
		&= -\tfrac12 \delta\transpose \Lambda \delta + \delta\transpose (A\transpose b - \eta) - \tfrac12 \lvert b \rvert^2 \\
		&= -\tfrac12 \lvert W (\delta - m) \rvert^2 + \tfrac12 \lvert W m \rvert^2 - \tfrac12 \lvert b \rvert^2 ,
	\end{aligned}
$$

since $\Lambda m = A\transpose b - \eta$. The first term is
$\logterm_{\mathcal N}(\delta;\, m, W) - \sum_i \log W_{ii} + \tfrac{D}{2} \log 2\pi$
by [](eq:math/normal/log-density) on $\R^D$, where the log-volume vanishes, and it is integrable.
Adding $c$ gives $c_{\mathcal N}$.

</Proof>

So the factors of full rank are the normal densities of the stacked tangent, and the improper
factors are everything else of the same shape. Integrability in $\delta$ is the only difference
between them, and a likelihood factor never needed it. Integrability in $\delta$ is also not
integrability against the reference measure of the points' own spaces, which carries the chart's
volume: on a compact curved space such as the rotations, a bounded factor of any rank is
integrable against the space's measure, and on a positive space the two differ by the factor
$\point$. What matters below is the reading in $\delta$, where the factor is Gaussian or flat.

What integrability does change is drawing. A quantity whose only information is an improper
factor has no law to be drawn from, and is a
[parameter with a score](../generative-models/#parameters-and-improper-priors). Where the factor
has full rank over Euclidean points, drawing $\delta$ from the normal of
[%full](statement:full-rank) and setting $\point_i = p_i \oplus \delta_i$ is a proposal under which
every draw has the same [weight](../importance-weights/).

## Two examples

**A bearing in the plane.** Take a landmark $\ell \in \R^2$ and poses $\point_k \in \R^2$, each with
its own prior $\mathcal N(p_k, P_k)$, and a measurement of each pose along a known unit direction
$a_k$,

$$
	z_k = a_k\transpose (\ell - \point_k) + \varepsilon_k , \qquad \varepsilon_k \sim \mathcal N(0, \sigma^2) ,
$$

a linear stand-in for a bearing, which measures the landmark's offset from the pose across one
direction only. Integrate the pose $\point_k$ out, with $z_k$ observed at $\hat z_k$. Then
$a_k\transpose \ell - z_k$ is normal with mean $a_k\transpose p_k$ and variance
$s_k^2 = \sigma^2 + a_k\transpose P_k a_k$, so that, with the anchor $q$ for $\ell$ and
$\delta = \ell - q$,

$$
	\logterm_k(\ell) = -\frac{1}{2 s_k^2} \bigl(a_k\transpose \delta - (\hat z_k + a_k\transpose p_k - a_k\transpose q)\bigr)^2 - \tfrac12 \log (2\pi s_k^2) .
$$

This is the factor with one row, $A = a_k\transpose / s_k$, $b = (\hat z_k + a_k\transpose p_k -
a_k\transpose q) / s_k$, $\eta = 0$ and $c = -\tfrac12 \log (2\pi s_k^2)$, exactly. It has rank one
on $\R^2$ and is constant along the direction orthogonal to $a_k$: the line on which the
measurement places the landmark. A second pose with a direction $a_{k'}$ not parallel to $a_k$
gives a second such factor. Their sum has two rows of rank two, and is by
[%full](statement:full-rank) a normal, whose mean is the point at which the two lines cross.

**A positive quantity.** Let a positive quantity $\point$ have the normal on the positive reals
with the mean $e^{\theta + \varphi}$ and the scale $\sigma$, where $\theta \sim \mathcal N(0, 1)$ is
real, and integrate $\theta$ out, keeping $\varphi$ and $\point$. The normal's log-density on the
positive reals is the Gaussian of $\log \point$ less the log-volume $\log \point$, so

$$
	\begin{aligned}
		\logterm_B(\varphi, \point)
		&= \log \int \mathcal N(\theta;\, 0, 1)\, \frac{1}{\point}\, \mathcal N(\log \point;\, \theta + \varphi, \sigma^2) \, \dd \theta \\
		&= -\log \point - \frac{(\log \point - \varphi)^2}{2 s^2} - \tfrac12 \log (2\pi s^2) ,
	\end{aligned}
$$

with $s^2 = 1 + \sigma^2$. In the stacked tangent at the anchors $(\hat \varphi, \hat \point)$,
$\delta = (\varphi - \hat \varphi,\, \log \point - \log \hat \point)$, it is the factor with

$$
	A = \tfrac{1}{s} \begin{bmatrix} -1 & 1 \end{bmatrix} , \qquad
	b = -\frac{\log \hat \point - \hat \varphi}{s} , \qquad
	\eta = (0, 1) , \qquad
	c = -\log \hat \point - \tfrac12 \log (2\pi s^2) .
$$

The linear term is not in the row space of $A$: by part 3 of [%full](statement:forms) below, at
most its projection onto that space, $(-\tfrac12, \tfrac12)$, can be moved into $b$, and
$(\tfrac12, \tfrac12)$ remains. Along the null direction $(1, 1)$ the factor falls linearly. This
is why the factor carries $\eta$: a log-volume that depends on the point and not only on a
residual, as the positive reals' does, leaves a slope that no row can hold.

## What elimination leaves

The two examples were integrals done by hand. In general, integrating a block of variables out of
a sum of squared rows and linear terms is part 2 of
[%full](statement:math/square-root-elimination/eliminate-one): with the notation of that lemma,

$$
	\log \int e^{-\phi(\tangent_k, \tangent_S)}\, \dd \tangent_k
	= -\tfrac12 \lvert A' \tangent_S - b' \rvert^2 - (\eta_S - T\transpose y)\transpose \tangent_S
	- \gamma + \tfrac{d_k}{2} \log 2\pi - \log \lvert \det R \rvert . @tag(eliminated)
$$

That is an improper Gaussian factor of the variables left: its rows are the rows the
factorization did not use, its linear term is what is left of the linear terms, and its constant
collects the rest. Nothing requires $A'$ to have full rank, and when the eliminated block was the
only thing tying down some direction of $\tangent_S$, it has not. The factor is the exact answer
all the same.

**The Kalman filter.** Take the scalar chain

$$
	\randomPoint_0 \sim \mathcal N(a, s^2), \quad
	\bfz_0 \mid \randomPoint_0 \sim \mathcal N(\randomPoint_0, r^2), \quad
	\randomPoint_1 \mid \randomPoint_0 \sim \mathcal N(\randomPoint_0, q^2) ,
$$

and integrate $\randomPoint_0$ out with $\bfz_0$ observed at $\hat z_0$. Completing the square in
$\point_0$ gives $\mathcal N(a, s^2)(\point_0)\, \mathcal N(\point_0, r^2)(\hat z_0) = \mathcal N(a,
s^2 + r^2)(\hat z_0)\, \mathcal N(\mu, P)(\point_0)$, writing $\mathcal N(m, v)(\point)$ for the
normal density of mean $m$ and variance $v$ at $\point$, and integrating the motion against it
gives

$$
	\logterm_B(\point_1) = \log \mathcal N(a, s^2 + r^2)(\hat z_0) + \log \mathcal N(\mu, P + q^2)(\point_1),
	\qquad
	K = \frac{s^2}{s^2 + r^2}, \quad \mu = a + K(\hat z_0 - a), \quad P = (1 - K)\, s^2 . @tag(kalman)
$$

The factor on $\point_1$ has full rank, and reads as two things. Its normal is the filter's
*prediction* of $\randomPoint_1$, made from the *update* $\mathcal N(\mu, P)$ of $\randomPoint_0$
by the measurement, with $K$ the gain. Its constant is the *evidence*, the density of the
measurement that was integrated over. A Kalman filter is this elimination repeated along a chain,
one state at a time, and it is exact because every term is linear and Gaussian. The improper
factor is what the same elimination gives when a state is not yet determined by what has been
measured, and the filter's covariance does not exist.

## Forms of one factor

<Statement {...forms} />

<Proof>

For part 1, $\lvert Q\transpose (A \delta - b) \rvert = \lvert A \delta - b \rvert$. For part 2, the
squares of the zero rows are $\lvert 0 \cdot \delta - e \rvert^2 = \lvert e \rvert^2$. For part 3,
expanding the square,

$$
	\begin{aligned}
		-\tfrac12 \lvert A \delta - (b - u) \rvert^2 - (\eta - A\transpose u)\transpose \delta
		&= -\tfrac12 \lvert A \delta - b \rvert^2 - u\transpose (A \delta - b) - \tfrac12 \lvert u \rvert^2 - \eta\transpose \delta + u\transpose A \delta \\
		&= -\tfrac12 \lvert A \delta - b \rvert^2 - \eta\transpose \delta + u\transpose b - \tfrac12 \lvert u \rvert^2 ,
	\end{aligned}
$$

and the new constant restores $c$. For the anchors,
$\point_i \ominus p_i' = (\point_i \ominus p_i) - w_i$ by [](eq:math/normal/translation),
and the blocks of the anchors kept are unchanged, so $\delta = \delta' + w$ in the new stacked
tangent $\delta'$, and substituting gives the new right-hand side and constant.

</Proof>

Parts 1 and 2 together are an orthogonal factorization of $[A \;\, b]$. It leaves at most $D$
rows, so a factor never needs more rows than its points have coordinates. Part 3 moves any part of
$\eta$ that lies in the row space into $b$. Choosing $u$ to minimize $\lvert \eta - A\transpose u
\rvert$ leaves only the part of $\eta$ in the null space, which no form removes. When $A$ has rank
$D$, that part is zero, and the form is the normal's of [%full](statement:full-rank). None of
these changes the value: a factor is the function it is, whichever form it is given in.

## Accumulation

Factors over the same points add. Two factors over sets of points $B_1$ and $B_2$, with the same
anchor for each point they share, sum to a factor over $B_1 \cup B_2$, whose rows are the two sets
of rows, each with zero columns for the points it does not involve, whose linear term is the sum of
the two, each extended by zeros, and whose constant is the sum. The sum's value is the sum of the
values, at every point, by the definition alone. Its rows can then be reduced by
[%full](statement:forms) to at most $D$, and once they have rank $D$ it is a normal.

The sum is exact when the anchors agree, and whatever the anchors of the points of spaces flat in
disguise, since the last part of [%full](statement:forms) moves those anchors without changing the
factor's value. On a curved space, the stacked tangent at one anchor is not an affine function of
the stacked tangent at another, and two factors anchored differently are two functions, not one
factor.

The example of the bearings shows what accumulation buys. A filter that drops each pose as it is
done with it is left with one row on the landmark each time. The landmark is undetermined until
two rows of different directions have accumulated, and until then a question that needs it
determined has no unique answer. After that it is estimated, and nothing was lost on the way.

## Derivatives

The factor is a function of its points through $\delta$, and its
[derivatives in charts](../derivatives-in-charts/) are those of its two parts. In the chart at
$\point_i$, the block of $\delta$ for $\point_i$ has the derivative $\D(\point_i \ominus p_i)$,
which is the identity at $\point_i = p_i$ and everywhere on a space flat in disguise, and on a
curved space moves with $\point_i$. So the residual $A \delta - b$ has the Jacobian $A\, \D \delta$,
and the linear part has the gradient $\D \delta\transpose \eta$.

On the spaces flat in disguise, $\delta$ is affine in every chart of its points, so the factor is
exactly quadratic in it. Its residual's Jacobian is constant, the matrix $\D \delta\transpose
A\transpose A\, \D \delta$ is its exact negative Hessian, and the linear part has no curvature. On
a curved space the factor has the curvature that $\delta$ has: it vanishes at the anchors and is of
first order in $\delta$ near them.
